And again, research has shown that individuals with extremely high IQs

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:10.100
tend to have themselves a hard time relating to the people around them as a result.

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:12.900
So Omni-Man is that, but times a thousand.

00:13:12.900 --> 00:13:13.500
Literally.

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:17.500
As he deals with more intelligence than any human has ever had or ever will.

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:19.700
How could he possibly see humans as equals

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:21.900
if he's talking about conquering foreign planets

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:24.900
and we're all busy thinking about the latest season of Selling Sunsets?

00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:29.100
In short, Omni-Man is literally primed to be the exact person that we see in Invincible.

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:31.100
He's a hyper-intelligent, god-like being

00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:35.300
surrounded by inferior life forms with significantly shorter lives and smaller brains.

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:36.900
And worst of all, he's getting older,

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:39.100
and his brain is becoming less and less flexible,

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:41.300
ensuring he is forever stuck in his ways.

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:44.300
Which then begs the question that we started this whole thing off with,

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:45.500
is there any hope for Mark?

00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:47.900
Sure, right now he's holding his ground to protect the human race,

00:13:47.900 --> 00:13:50.500
but if we were to fast-forward 3,000 years for him,

00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:53.900
would Mark become just as cruel and heartless as his dear old dad?

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:58.100
Well, believe it or not, but the answer actually comes down to another superhero on the roster.

00:13:58.100 --> 00:14:01.900
In the lore of the series, Viltrumites aren't the only long-lived characters.

00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:03.500
Case in point, the Immortal.

00:14:03.500 --> 00:14:05.900
If you couldn't tell by the name, the Immortal cannot die.

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:08.300
Or, at least when he does die, it tends not to stick.

00:14:08.300 --> 00:14:09.300
He always comes back.

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:11.700
The Immortal can heal from basically any wound,

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:13.100
and he seems to never age.

00:14:13.100 --> 00:14:16.500
We see that the Immortal actually predates most of human civilization,

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:19.700
gaining his powers back during the Bronze Age of 3300 BC

00:14:19.700 --> 00:14:23.100
before living out lifetimes as a medieval knight in 580.

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:26.700
And even as President Abraham Lincoln in the 1860s.

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:28.500
Yeah, that's a weird creative choice.

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:30.300
Anyway, considering he's been alive this long,

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:33.300
you would expect him to fall into the same sorts of issues that Nolan has,

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:37.900
except the Immortal clearly isn't cruel or apathetic in the same way Nolan is.

00:14:37.900 --> 00:14:39.500
So, what's the difference here?

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:41.500
What's the X-factor for the Immortal?

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:43.500
And how is that gonna apply to Mark?

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:45.500
It actually boils down to three things.

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:48.100
The first is the way that the Immortal's powers seem to work.

00:14:48.100 --> 00:14:52.100
While Viltrumites like Mark and Nolan are long-lived and can heal from pretty bad injuries

00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:54.500
thanks to the smart atoms that are floating around in their bodies,

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:57.900
they can't come back from fatal blows like the Immortal can.

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:01.900
We see tons of Viltrumites dying both in the comics and in the show's flashbacks,

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:05.100
but we see the Immortal come back from practically everything

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:09.500
in a way that's much more akin to the classic healing factor that you see with other comic book superheroes.

00:15:09.500 --> 00:15:14.100
That means that he's constantly replenishing all the various subsets of cells within his body,

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:16.300
which would include the neurons in his brain.

00:15:16.300 --> 00:15:18.500
Unlike a typical human or Viltrumite brain,

00:15:18.500 --> 00:15:21.500
where neurons tend to hit a wall before the numbers slowly decline,

00:15:21.500 --> 00:15:24.300
the Immortal's brain is constantly being replenished,

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:27.900
meaning that it's always staying that nice, hot, moldable plastic.

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:32.100
Like a youngster, the Immortal would be able to quickly adapt to changing circumstances,

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:35.500
also explaining how he's able to master the hundreds of languages and cultures

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:37.700
that he's lived through during his extended lifespan.

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:40.700
This one, sadly, doesn't really apply to our Mark example,

00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:44.500
but it had to be called out because it is a significant factor in his success.

00:15:44.500 --> 00:15:48.500
The second factor that sets the Immortal apart from Nolan is his relationships.

00:15:48.500 --> 00:15:52.300
In the character handbook, it clearly states that the Immortal's relationships with other heroes

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:57.100
has given him a renewed passion for life and established closer connections with friendships and teammates.

00:15:57.100 --> 00:15:58.900
He's in partnership with Dupli-Kate.

00:15:58.900 --> 00:16:00.700
He's leader of the Guardians of the Globe.

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:03.900
The Immortal has a sincere kinship with the people around him,

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:05.900
despite his incredibly advanced age,

00:16:05.900 --> 00:16:09.100
and this adheres to what we read about in real-life science.

00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:13.300
Studies into the factors that give people a greater sense of empathy deeper into their lives

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:16.900
show time and time again that social interaction is key.

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:19.900
Spending time with friends, building and maintaining social connections,

00:16:19.900 --> 00:16:23.100
they are some of the best things that you can do for your brain's health.

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:25.900
It exercises that brain's empathy muscle.

00:16:25.900 --> 00:16:29.500
But if those muscles aren't worked out, well, they start to atrophy and die.

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:35.100
Case in point, Omni-Man, a loner who never truly buys into the whole team dynamic of the Guardians.

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:38.300
He is sent by the Viltrumites to Earth, a forgotten foreign planet,

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:40.100
and he's stationed there alone.

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:42.700
He's a sole soldier waiting for the right time to strike.

00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:46.300
He is isolated completely on a planet full of aliens.

00:16:46.300 --> 00:16:49.700
Even his cover job as an author emphasizes this loneliness.

00:16:49.700 --> 00:16:52.500
He's not going into the office alongside a bunch of other co-workers,

00:16:52.500 --> 00:16:54.900
he's at home alone writing books.

00:16:54.900 --> 00:16:59.500
Every element of his existence has primed him to lack empathy.

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:03.500
So Mark, spending time with William, Eve, and Amber, other superheroes like the Teen Team,

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:05.700
that is the sort of thing that's gonna keep him grounded.

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:09.700
Those relationships would presumably help him stay firmly rooted on the side of good

00:17:09.700 --> 00:17:13.500
and not join the evil space mustache empire years later.

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:17.700
He is starting off with a good, solid foundation of empathy as a kid,

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:20.100
which segues nicely into important point number three,

00:17:20.100 --> 00:17:24.100
the most important thing that the Immortal and Mark share, being born on Earth.

00:17:24.100 --> 00:17:27.500
The peak time for children to learn empathy is before the age of nine,

00:17:27.500 --> 00:17:29.300
and they learn it by watching others.

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:32.500
As babies, we can feel and respond to the emotions of our caregivers.

00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:36.900
As toddlers, we learn more about how others feel, and we might try to comfort them when they're upset.

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:39.700
As we grow older, especially in preschool and early school years,

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:42.100
we get better at understanding different emotions,

00:17:42.100 --> 00:17:45.900
and realizing that people might see things differently than our perspective.

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:50.300
By middle childhood, we become even more skilled at understanding others' feelings and needs.

00:17:50.300 --> 00:17:54.100
It keeps developing as we grow up, but our foundational understanding of it

00:17:54.100 --> 00:17:56.700
has to happen in these early childhood years.

00:17:56.700 --> 00:17:58.100
The Immortal? He was born a human.

00:17:58.100 --> 00:18:01.100
He grew up with the social customs and teachings of a human race

00:18:01.100 --> 00:18:03.100
where empathy is an important part of survival.

00:18:03.100 --> 00:18:04.500
Mark? Same story.

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:09.100
He has a compassionate human mother and a dad that, you know, well, not naturally feeling himself,

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:12.900
at least pretended to be for the sake of appearing normal to the outside world.

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:16.900
As such, Mark, he shouldn't be stunted in his empathetic education as he grows older.

00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:19.700
By contrast, Nolan grew up on Viltrum,

00:18:19.700 --> 00:18:23.300
where whole swaths of the population were wiped out for being seen as too weak.

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:29.100
As a result, he never truly received that early childhood exposure to the concept of compassion and empathy.

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:32.900
Ironically enough, a man who has the lifespan long enough to learn about anything

00:18:32.900 --> 00:18:36.900
can't truly embrace this one essential lesson because he missed it as a kid.

00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:38.900
That said, clearly Nolan's not without hope.

00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:42.500
In a flashback to Mark's childhood, Debbie explains that as people grow older,

00:18:42.500 --> 00:18:45.500
they forget to experience the joy and excitement of the world around them.

00:18:45.500 --> 00:18:49.100
But that children can be the vehicle for adults to remember all that.

00:18:49.100 --> 00:18:53.900
A way for them to feel empathy again and to see that life can truly be fulfilling and interesting.

00:18:53.900 --> 00:18:55.900
And the thing that finally breaks Omni-Man?

00:18:55.900 --> 00:18:59.700
Mark's answer to the very question that started us down this rabbit hole in the first place.

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:03.900
What will you have after 500 years?

00:19:04.900 --> 00:19:08.900
You, Dad. I'd still have you.

00:19:08.900 --> 00:19:12.900
It's Nolan's love for his son Mark over his sense of duty to the Empire.

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:16.500
This shred of empathy and compassion that finally breaks through.

00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:18.500
That's what's so beautiful about this answer.

00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:21.700
Mark being the thing that grounds his father, that makes him have a change of heart,

00:19:21.700 --> 00:19:24.700
it isn't just a powerful piece of Invincible's thematic storytelling,

00:19:24.700 --> 00:19:27.500
this is a real scientifically backed phenomenon.

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:31.500
One that shows us that we're never too old to learn, to feel, to help others.

00:19:31.700 --> 00:19:34.300
That right there, that is the true superpower.

00:19:34.300 --> 00:19:37.500
An emotional strength that can truly make you invincible.

00:19:37.500 --> 00:19:41.100
Unless, you know, someone actually attacks you with a weapon, in which case, no, you're not.

00:19:41.100 --> 00:19:42.300
You're just done.

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:45.100
But, hey, that's just a theory.

00:19:45.100 --> 00:19:47.100
A...

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:49.100
And cut.

